{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Phase 1 RCA (rebar-debug) — mechanism PROVEN; disposition is a scope decision for a human\n\n### Confirmed mechanism (runtime-verified, not read off the source)\n\n`ProcessHistory.before_model_request` DOES run before every model request, including the\nfirst, and receives the exact outgoing wire. The seam is therefore *not* misplaced — the hook\nfires at the right moment. What fails is the **filter**: `_make_wire_projection`'s\n`proc(messages)` (`src/rebar/llm/pai_retry.py:209-218`) only descends into a message when it is\n`isinstance(msg, ModelResponse) and _is_failed_output_response(msg)`, and\n`_wire_keeps_response` (`:176-194`) is the **only** site that raises\n`ContextWindowExceededError` (`:189`). On a first request the history is `[ModelRequest]`, so\nthat predicate never matches and the raise site is unreachable.\n\n**Experiment 1 (dynamic, runtime oracle).** Drove the real `PydanticAIRunner` over an offline\n`FunctionModel` (`ALLOW_MODEL_REQUESTS=False`) with 3,600,000 chars of `instructions` against a\nresolved candidate window of 1,000,000 tokens, with spies on `_make_wire_projection` and\n`_wire_keeps_response`. Observed:\n\n- `proc` invocations: `[['ModelRequest']]` — invoked once, saw only a request\n- `_wire_keeps_response` invocations: **0**\n- model calls: **1** — 3,604,182 chars (~901k est. tokens) handed to the provider\n- no error raised; the run returned a normal verdict\n\nThis is the decisive discrimination: the guard's silence on an oversized first request is not a\n\"fits\" verdict, it is the guard never executing.\n\n**Experiment 2 (today's failure mode, empirically established rather than assumed).** Scripted\nthe model to raise `ModelHTTPError(400, body=\"prompt is too long: 206826 tokens > 200000\nmaximum\")`. Observed surface:\n\n```\nrebar.llm.errors.LLMUnavailableError:\n  the LLM provider call failed: status_code: 400, ... 'prompt is too long: ...'\n```\n\nOne provider round-trip burned. So an oversized first request surfaces as\n**\"the LLM provider call failed\"** — provider-outage semantics for a deterministic,\ncaller-fixable input problem. Corroborated statically: no transport retry (400 is absent from\n`anthropic_model.py:77` `_RETRY_STATUSES`), no fallback-chain retry (`model_classes.py:341-368`\nexcludes `CHANGE_INPUT`), but plan-review's size ladder *does* burn a full failed round-trip per\nrung (`plan_review/sizing.py:524-525`, `:551-552`). The correct disposition\n(`CHANGE_INPUT`, `retryable=False`) is already computed at `failure.py:214` and attached at\n`run_failure.py:163-165` — it is the *type* that is wrong, not the classification.\n\n### Attribution — OMISSION, never a regression\n\n`git log -L 176,220:src/rebar/llm/pai_retry.py` returns **exactly one commit**. The file is new\nin it (`git show --stat`: `pai_retry.py | 232 +++++`). Line-scoped\n`git blame -w -C -M -L 160,225` attributes the filter predicate (`:214`) and the whole of\n`_wire_keeps_response` to:\n\n- **`012aef629cb1a0814099e8bca0fec85bae1831b6`** — \"RP-01 S2: bounded pydantic-ai structured\n  op\", 2026-08-16, `rebar-ticket: 66b3-4214-c70b-4a9a` (`kingsize-unfair-blackbird`).\n\nThe one apparent second author (`72e59d982f` on lines 210-211) is a `-C -M` copy-detection\nartifact: at `012aef629c^` that identical `from pydantic_ai.messages import ModelResponse` line\nlives in an unrelated `runner.py` helper; no wire projection existed there. Corroborating\nsweeps: `git log --all -S'_wire_keeps_response'`, `-S'ContextWindowExceededError'`, and\n`-S'ProcessHistory'` over `src/rebar/` all reduce to `012aef629c` (plus `cd91e39c4a`, a\npre-rebase duplicate with the same Change-Id, not an ancestor of HEAD).\n\n**Date test:** the implicated lines and the behaviour's origin are the same commit, so there is\nno earlier shape in which a first request was measured. This is an **omission** — the case was\nnever implemented — not a regression. Attribution is therefore **by scope-of-work** to\n`66b3-4214-c70b-4a9a`, the story that established the guard and whose acceptance criteria\ndefined its reach. `caused_by` recorded. (This is *not* the whole-file-blame default: a\nwhole-file blame would name the same commit here only by coincidence, since the file has one\nauthor.)\n\n### Why Phase 2 is NOT unlocked — the repair exceeds a bug fix's authorisation\n\nThe parent brief named two possibilities. The evidence lands on the second, and it is worth\nbeing precise about *which* part is out of scope: the seam is correctly placed, but the check\ncannot be made to cover the first request without three additions that a bug fix is not\nauthorised to make.\n\n**1. There is no measurement to filter differently to.** The existing check does not measure\nmessage content at all — it reads `response.usage.input_tokens`, the provider's *already-billed*\ncount from the previous turn (`_response_output_tokens`, `:143-156`). A `ModelRequest` carries\nno usage. So this is not a mis-filter that can be corrected by widening a predicate; covering\nthe first request requires introducing a **local token estimator** at this seam, which is\nmeasuring something the current check does not measure.\n\n**2. The estimator's calibration is load-bearing across ops, and the codebase disagrees with\nitself about it.** Four independent estimators exist today and they differ by 2x:\n`enrich._CONTEXT_CHARS_PER_TOKEN = 2` (`enrich.py:32`),\n`completion_criteria._CONTEXT_CHARS_PER_TOKEN = 2` (`:42`),\n`det_floor.CHARS_PER_TOKEN = 4` (`:104`), plus two inline `len(s)//4 + 1` lambdas\n(`plan_review/__init__.py:188`, `evals/eval_solver.py:183`). This is not cosmetic: `enrich`\nbounds its source to `own_window_tokens(model) * 2` **chars** (`enrich.py:90`). A seam estimator\nat chars/2 would score a maximally-bounded enrich payload at exactly `window` tokens, and\n`window + reserve > window` fails closed — **breaking AC#3 of this very ticket** (enrich's\nnow-successful truncated calls turned back into hard failures). At chars/4 it scores\n`window/2` and passes. Picking the constant *is* the design decision, and it decides whether an\nop keeps working.\n\n**3. The posture decision is open, by this ticket's own admission.** The Scope section says\n\"Decide the posture per op class … A one-size posture is probably wrong; the seam may need to\nraise a typed error the op can choose to catch\", and \"Consider whether the existing per-op\nbounds should then defer to the shared seam\" — both marked \"to be confirmed by whoever claims\nthis\". Those are design questions, not a defect's repair.\n\n### Design authority says the seam was scoped to the retry wire deliberately\n\n- Ticket `66b3-4214-c70b-4a9a`, Approach §1 (verbatim): the rule fires on \"**that response's own\n  `response.usage.input_tokens + output_tokens`** (the tokens it already billed)\". Every one of\n  its acceptance criteria is phrased over \"a failed response\" / the retry wire. **No criterion,\n  and no sentence in the ticket, names the first or outgoing request.**\n- The held-out oracle encodes the same scoping: `test_authoritative_input_over_window_raises_\n  context_window_exceeded` (`tests/unit/test_rp01_s2_bounded_op_oracle.py:240-251`) can only\n  produce the overflow by scripting a *model response* with `usage=(window + 1, 5)` — i.e. after\n  a round-trip has already happened.\n- Most decisive, and recent: the fix for the sibling bug — `0076113ee8`, 2026-08-21, five days\n  ago — **explicitly considered extending this seam and deliberately declined** (verbatim from\n  its commit message): \"Truncating here is consistent with `pai_retry`'s 'fail closed rather\n  than SHORTEN authoritative content': that rule scopes to the authoritative retry wire of a\n  VERIFICATION op … The ticket-digest source is lossy summarizer input … so a digest from a\n  truncated source is a slightly weaker dedup hint, while failing closed loses the feature\n  entirely on the tickets that most need it.\" A deliberate, in-window decision for the op-local\n  posture over the shared-seam posture is current design intent, not stale code.\n\nThe one authority pointing the other way, stated fairly: `errors.py:43-48` describes the error's\ntrigger in **request** terms — \"the next request's authoritative input (system/user/tool\ncontent) plus the output reserve exceeds every viable candidate model's context window\". A first\nrequest's system+user content is exactly that, so the error type's documented meaning is broader\nthan the implementation's reachability. That is a real argument that the guard under-delivers\nagainst its own error's contract. It is *not* an argument that the repair is small: it still\nrequires all three additions above.\n\n### Blast radius (caller-dependency sweep)\n\n15 modules reach this seam via `runner.py:514 -> apply_structured_seams -> wire_history_processor`\n(findings, enrich, epic_bug_screen, eval_solver, plan_review x5, workflow x2, overlap/judge,\ncode_review x2). `ContextWindowExceededError` has **zero consumers** outside this module and its\noracle test. Containment is partly by inheritance — it is an `LLMRunnerError`/`LLMError`, and\ntoday's `LLMUnavailableError` is also an `LLMError`, so the 13 broad `except LLMError` handlers\ncatch either. The behavioural risk is therefore *not* the type swap; it is calls that succeed\ntoday and would be newly refused by a conservative estimator (point 2 above).\n\n### Classification: `feature-request` (a shared-seam design change), NOT `defect`\n\nThe gap is real, empirically demonstrated, and worth doing. But no authoritative source\nestablishes that this seam was *meant* to guard the first request; the story that built it\nscoped the check to carried responses in its approach and its acceptance criteria, its oracle\nencodes that scoping, and the only recent decision on the question chose the op-local posture\ninstead. Per rebar-debug's not-a-bug guard this does not unlock automatic RED->GREEN repair, and\nper the standing scope rule a bug fix restores documented behaviour rather than widening it.\n\n**No code change was made. No test was written. Phase 2 was not entered.**\n\n### Recommended disposition (for a human to choose)\n\nRe-scope this from `bug` to a **story/task** and answer its three open questions in a plan\nbefore implementation, in this order:\n\n1. **Calibration** — adopt ONE shared chars-per-token constant for pre-flight estimation, or a\n   real tokenizer. Must be chosen so `enrich`'s `window * 2`-char bound stays admissible\n   (chars/4 does; chars/2 does not).\n2. **Posture** — fail-closed `ContextWindowExceededError` for verification ops vs. an\n   op-catchable typed error for lossy-summarizer ops. The `0076113ee8` reasoning is the input to\n   this decision, not an obstacle to it.\n3. **Convergence** — whether the four existing per-op bounds then defer to the shared seam, or\n   remain as first-line bounds with the seam as backstop.\n\nA cheaper, strictly-narrower intermediate that *would* fit a bug fix and is worth considering\nseparately: `run_failure.py:152` already has the `CHANGE_INPUT` classification in hand when it\nwraps the provider error, yet raises `LLMUnavailableError` (\"the LLM provider call failed\"). A\n`CHANGE_INPUT` 400 could be re-typed to a rebar error that says \"your input is too big\" without\nany estimator, any calibration choice, or any new measurement — it only re-types an error rebar\nhas *already correctly classified*. That does not satisfy this ticket's AC#1 (\"before the\nprovider call\"), so it is a different ticket, not a substitute — flagged here rather than acted\non.\n\n### Residual risk observed but not fixed\n\n- **Duplicated context-limit phrase lists.** `failure.py:144-157` `_CONTEXT_LEN_HINTS` and\n  `plan_review/sizing.py:313-329` `is_context_limit_error` maintain the same phrase list\n  independently; `failure.py:144` itself notes \"the two must stay in sync\". Disposition:\n  **accepted, not filed** — pre-existing, unrelated to this mechanism, and visible in the code\n  with a comment already warning about it.\n- **Four divergent chars-per-token constants (2 vs 4).** Disposition: **folded into\n  recommendation 1 above** — it is the substance of this ticket's calibration decision, not a\n  separate finding."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787311778312567001,"uuid":"bb3ec457-b83e-4aae-918a-c78fbd6d4c21"}